@startuml release_flow
title Cutting a release, and how a user installs (docs/RELEASING.md)

skinparam shadowing false

actor Maintainer
participant "github.com/paddock-sh/paddock" as Repo
participant ".github/workflows/release.yml" as Actions
participant "GitHub release vX.Y.Z" as Release
database PyPI
actor User
participant "install.sh" as Install
participant "uv" as Uv

== Cut ==

Maintainer -> Repo: push tag vX.Y.Z\n(version bumped in pyproject.toml\nand paddock/~__init~__.py)
Repo -> Actions: tag push
activate Actions
Actions -> Actions: uv run pytest
Actions -> Actions: uv build\n(sdist + wheel)
Actions -> Actions: tag matches the built version?\ntwine check dist/*
Actions -> Release: gh release create --generate-notes\nwith both files attached
Actions -> PyPI: pypa/gh-action-pypi-publish\n(trusted publishing, no stored token)
note right of Actions
  The PyPI job is skipped while the
  PYPI_PUBLISH repository variable is
  unset. The name "paddock" is taken on
  PyPI, so nothing is published yet.
end note
deactivate Actions

== Install ==

User -> Install: curl -LsSf raw.githubusercontent.com/.../install.sh | sh
activate Install
note right of Install
  Trust boundary: this runs code fetched
  over the network, on the user's machine,
  as the user. The script is short so it
  can be read before it is run.
end note
Install -> Uv: uv tool install --force\ngit+https://github.com/paddock-sh/paddock
Uv -> Repo: clone the default branch\n(PADDOCK_REF picks another)
Install -> User: next: paddock init, then prefix+c
deactivate Install

note across
  The install path goes to the repo, not to the release
  or to PyPI. Until that changes, what users get is
  whatever is on the default branch.
end note

@enduml
